e vector $[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}]$ has both properties." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-94005/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a6598e4e-7ea6-56ce-abef-95e64d010e96"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-94005"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-94035/selection/060233fb4e5193a1bb93ce9490c20c8be9663a878fe934bf044ced7797cdfd70" :answer/id #uuid "44c0bd11-e730-59f8-8fb8-deaa441bed5f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94035/selection/cb630d41e4e8d4d8923ca0df3c76cf87d627a018cf39103e01e825657fde0820" :answer/id #uuid "f6640f86-119d-5f16-a314-99249b431084" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94035/selection/b70163afa80242b6e07599db9e6649592c88c8df8d6892329736a0a5f2ef7d0f" :answer/id #uuid "e26bf280-17b0-5c1d-ad33-cd87a9ec0727" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94035/selection/60eadf18022e36714cee17e0479ab6af66a6361ceef7571ec8305b4e57ee6d8f" :answer/id #uuid "d952aeea-010f-55cb-afb4-8c7cd7c0ac31" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94035/selection/fafb5c68fd86714db50ceb86555eb72f36b3a566d9758baaf725db0d5f32b11a" :answer/id #uuid "517fe7b0-ede9-5cc2-bba3-ab51f073c91f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94035/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "1a631074-7259-582f-921d-21de94eb740e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-94035/selection/060233fb4e5193a1bb93ce9490c20c8be9663a878fe934bf044ced7797cdfd70" :answer-field/choices ["q-94035/selection/060233fb4e5193a1bb93ce9490c20c8be9663a878fe934bf044ced7797cdfd70" "q-94035/selection/cb630d41e4e8d4d8923ca0df3c76cf87d627a018cf39103e01e825657fde0820" "q-94035/selection/b70163afa80242b6e07599db9e6649592c88c8df8d6892329736a0a5f2ef7d0f" "q-94035/selection/60eadf18022e36714cee17e0479ab6af66a6361ceef7571ec8305b4e57ee6d8f" "q-94035/selection/fafb5c68fd86714db50ceb86555eb72f36b3a566d9758baaf725db0d5f32b11a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-94035" :question/id #uuid "01d6bc91-3005-5cb6-9f58-ca82d4ab3b4f" :question/math-academy-id "q-94035" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the first column of the matrix $P.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=6.$ So, the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4.$ Computing $A-(-4)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+4I & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 4\\end{aligned}]+4[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 8 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-2{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{5}.$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm, we obtain the first column of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-94035/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "31ae5cdc-ae3f-5497-88d8-f02a7263340d"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-94035"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-94032/selection/c25b4c5a34c9f50ba5cab12d26d91674670de71293237dd889d3975aa50ccc8f" :answer/id #uuid "7a6383a7-d3db-5fd2-a40d-3390587cf190" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94032/selection/83e1afda14b7828dd32335bb4b02d9e461e6f35eda76b7346cb6b7a1789f445c" :answer/id #uuid "cfba9fd1-2035-55ef-aa7c-4c4cf56f7bda" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94032/selection/9753ab08599301025dae2c5b4a8b35946341fa141cafbe05acc198a976b8d87e" :answer/id #uuid "7f5dc606-86fb-5767-b47e-fdcc5bb061d7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3\\sqrt{13}}{13} \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{13}}{13}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94032/selection/c82f85743432ad7e3c8a7730d832d04a2baf59e50b355a990f0603aa7c669cdf" :answer/id #uuid "cc2ab260-e294-5bfc-b2bf-0e264cfaddfa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\sqrt{13}}{13} \\\\ -\\frac{3\\sqrt{13}}{13}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94032/selection/13d66094b8248e6fa8a4f88c42d3eeda247b79a227dcc4cf0d7b7e9ad930366f" :answer/id #uuid "fb700f59-18b6-5a41-9fe3-6393db88e7ce" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\sqrt{13}}{13} \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{13}}{13}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94032/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "811611eb-fe64-5aa2-a626-d93b022ae577" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-94032/selection/13d66094b8248e6fa8a4f88c42d3eeda247b79a227dcc4cf0d7b7e9ad930366f" :answer-field/choices ["q-94032/selection/c25b4c5a34c9f50ba5cab12d26d91674670de71293237dd889d3975aa50ccc8f" "q-94032/selection/83e1afda14b7828dd32335bb4b02d9e461e6f35eda76b7346cb6b7a1789f445c" "q-94032/selection/9753ab08599301025dae2c5b4a8b35946341fa141cafbe05acc198a976b8d87e" "q-94032/selection/c82f85743432ad7e3c8a7730d832d04a2baf59e50b355a990f0603aa7c669cdf" "q-94032/selection/13d66094b8248e6fa8a4f88c42d3eeda247b79a227dcc4cf0d7b7e9ad930366f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-94032" :question/id #uuid "e266e00f-9fa3-5a93-849a-c0c7dd5a8f68" :question/math-academy-id "q-94032" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -10\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the first column of the matrix $P.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-10.$ So, the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=3.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{1}=3.$ Computing $A-3I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -1\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{2}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=3,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{13}.$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm, we obtain the first column of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\sqrt{13}}{13} \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{13}}{13}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-94032/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "31ae5cdc-ae3f-5497-88d8-f02a7263340d"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-94032"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-140825/selection/6f936a43e465d96f936ad2140968dd8781e33739c36279988ac46bf2320bb474" :answer/id #uuid "97ec03b2-c163-59d5-9bcd-84591551f85f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140825/selection/b70163afa80242b6e07599db9e6649592c88c8df8d6892329736a0a5f2ef7d0f" :answer/id #uuid "4e447fd3-4ab1-58ba-ad7f-5c31eab23ecd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140825/selection/a702c6045eecfae8cb23a6d8894d3e548e95386f70f6cd1aed249146e1f1fd94" :answer/id #uuid "82618c0e-0826-5ff2-ba01-4a2eb5cf7832" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140825/selection/4afe72ce1ff2948bb282f72a051259b7c7c995391c6a8ae0e3d05b32a2584b91" :answer/id #uuid "39e54b22-2e2b-590c-adb6-a7fbc18c2131" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140825/selection/060233fb4e5193a1bb93ce9490c20c8be9663a878fe934bf044ced7797cdfd70" :answer/id #uuid "76d4b87b-989a-542b-b045-176902fedd08" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140825/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "4bd1dcf4-3f09-58bb-a9b3-b7454b8d744d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-140825/selection/060233fb4e5193a1bb93ce9490c20c8be9663a878fe934bf044ced7797cdfd70" :answer-field/choices ["q-140825/selection/6f936a43e465d96f936ad2140968dd8781e33739c36279988ac46bf2320bb474" "q-140825/selection/b70163afa80242b6e07599db9e6649592c88c8df8d6892329736a0a5f2ef7d0f" "q-140825/selection/a702c6045eecfae8cb23a6d8894d3e548e95386f70f6cd1aed249146e1f1fd94" "q-140825/selection/4afe72ce1ff2948bb282f72a051259b7c7c995391c6a8ae0e3d05b32a2584b91" "q-140825/selection/060233fb4e5193a1bb93ce9490c20c8be9663a878fe934bf044ced7797cdfd70"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-140825" :question/id #uuid "e21a37fd-af47-56e6-bbdf-7f961f5a8890" :question/math-academy-id "q-140825" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the second column of the matrix $P.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=6$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=1.$ So, the second column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=1.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{2}=1.$ Computing $A-I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-2{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{5}.$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm, we obtain the second column of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-140825/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "31ae5cdc-ae3f-5497-88d8-f02a7263340d"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-140825"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-94037/selection/5655218c50b3d33a204c6656a10ade40fadaa461bc392cbf9506d32ded668e79" :answer/id #uuid "b35c4888-dee7-5c0e-9bee-e9a92b738cc0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4\\sqrt{17}}{17} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{17}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94037/selection/84762459bef6f107ef2cbf2e830c8f92da9b281e07c47ed0412a20ac8689c76a" :answer/id #uuid "c83d7e4f-a53d-59b0-b8be-23ea53594dc8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94037/selection/c7d6333a50e7d7ee3ab36841c45806d884e876cb043e9bc82bfc0d285c31caab" :answer/id #uuid "fbee763b-f98d-58a3-ab19-124054876ddd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{17} \\\\ \\frac{4\\sqrt{17}}{17}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94037/selection/e96e1d3465e2c709d54a4bb8564b2bd9898dcadb3b73045259d0ca18ed338675" :answer/id #uuid "e9cc78ff-2779-597d-8ab8-3011092b2c54" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94037/selection/4ca612ad25a8027e77edd623dc725236b8e9c5cd017a0223023c83d132222def" :answer/id #uuid "47c2e06c-de78-5ace-bd8e-cf5e89a9fdc1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{17} \\\\ \\frac{4\\sqrt{17}}{17}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94037/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "af36f979-d3e9-5bee-9f77-1fe325401ffc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-94037/selection/4ca612ad25a8027e77edd623dc725236b8e9c5cd017a0223023c83d132222def" :answer-field/choices ["q-94037/selection/5655218c50b3d33a204c6656a10ade40fadaa461bc392cbf9506d32ded668e79" "q-94037/selection/84762459bef6f107ef2cbf2e830c8f92da9b281e07c47ed0412a20ac8689c76a" "q-94037/selection/c7d6333a50e7d7ee3ab36841c45806d884e876cb043e9bc82bfc0d285c31caab" "q-94037/selection/e96e1d3465e2c709d54a4bb8564b2bd9898dcadb3b73045259d0ca18ed338675" "q-94037/selection/4ca612ad25a8027e77edd623dc725236b8e9c5cd017a0223023c83d132222def"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-94037" :question/id #uuid "b5923242-1a47-5487-a3a8-4c37dcb336a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-94037" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}7 & -4 \\\\ -4 & -8\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -9\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the second column of the matrix $P.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=8$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-9.$ So, the second column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=-9.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{2}=-9.$ Computing $A-(-9)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+9I & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & -4 \\\\ -4 & -8\\end{aligned}]+9[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}16 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}16 & -4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}16 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain $4{x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ 4{x}_{1}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{1}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{17}.$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm, we obtain the second column of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{17} \\\\ \\frac{4\\sqrt{17}}{17}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-94037/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "31ae5cdc-ae3f-5497-88d8-f02a7263340d"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-94037"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-94039/selection/13d66094b8248e6fa8a4f88c42d3eeda247b79a227dcc4cf0d7b7e9ad930366f" :answer/id #uuid "e18c3aca-cd91-5982-be76-dbc3595cedf1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\sqrt{13}}{13} \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{13}}{13}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94039/selection/c82f85743432ad7e3c8a7730d832d04a2baf59e50b355a990f0603aa7c669cdf" :answer/id #uuid "de7d1cbe-0cde-5a3d-b5da-4a11cdf9d16e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\sqrt{13}}{13} \\\\ -\\frac{3\\sqrt{13}}{13}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94039/selection/64016bee11c110718ed4fa5affa1338833add0794a386797f22b2a6d8512dc99" :answer/id #uuid "6933fe82-6ff3-5eab-9c92-a47254138f58" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94039/selection/76dac6206f832bc210496b0c0beb51cb081b2897f2c7866b9ce0cdfa06483073" :answer/id #uuid "981b9790-cf11-552c-895e-8756a9c1c1eb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3\\sqrt{13}}{13} \\\\ -\\frac{2\\sqrt{13}}{13}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94039/selection/fe2a88bb40c84469c9d4347b9459a49a2043ff3a9a3fd859c6b18594ed560d4c" :answer/id #uuid "6c77a9be-395a-52e9-a008-f35adc1bbcc9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94039/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "28ca353c-33ec-5e0a-8a89-7638677ccf11" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-94039/selection/c82f85743432ad7e3c8a7730d832d04a2baf59e50b355a990f0603aa7c669cdf" :answer-field/choices ["q-94039/selection/13d66094b8248e6fa8a4f88c42d3eeda247b79a227dcc4cf0d7b7e9ad930366f" "q-94039/selection/c82f85743432ad7e3c8a7730d832d04a2baf59e50b355a990f0603aa7c669cdf" "q-94039/selection/64016bee11c110718ed4fa5affa1338833add0794a386797f22b2a6d8512dc99" "q-94039/selection/76dac6206f832bc210496b0c0beb51cb081b2897f2c7866b9ce0cdfa06483073" "q-94039/selection/fe2a88bb40c84469c9d4347b9459a49a2043ff3a9a3fd859c6b18594ed560d4c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-94039" :question/id #uuid "45313e55-9dc2-5f9b-a30e-26241f08ab4f" :question/math-academy-id "q-94039" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the second column of the matrix $P.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=8$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-5.$ So, the second column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=-5.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{2}=-5.$ Computing $A-(-5)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+5I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -1\\end{aligned}]+5[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-\\frac{2}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}9 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=-3,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{13}.$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm, we obtain the second column of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\sqrt{13}}{13} \\\\ -\\frac{3\\sqrt{13}}{13}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-94039/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "31ae5cdc-ae3f-5497-88d8-f02a7263340d"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-94039"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-140828/selection/f7285be8b4504c6a509b66bc43c0c03ff518b2b8247dc246b9fafccc664377a9" :answer/id #uuid "23fc848c-4225-59fb-9991-919fecb4cd69" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140828/selection/7ec8174158f1dabc26c4813f65e174a34fc509a8f3e15394c2e88587699d8f03" :answer/id #uuid "c34ed81e-7246-5047-b5f8-f44136b27b2a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140828/selection/80e131d9f4ac494bfb9e9219a2b78cb88c01dd691e748930d96e60184c1f6d3a" :answer/id #uuid "237b57d9-d4a4-5bb5-9de5-