tion/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe matrix $A$ above has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=5$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1.$ Find a diagonal matrix $D$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nNote that $A$ is symmetric. Therefore, it is orthogonally diagonalizable. Let's now go through the process.\n\n**Step 1.**  Notice that we already have the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=5\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-1$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-5I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]-5[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-5I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-1)I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]+1[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-1)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-140828/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4754d4e6-b2d0-5efb-ac92-c004c70004df"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-140828"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-94046/selection/27e238a304698a046c7e795fdbd029dfb56d969c17068e8ee401237d089bee46" :answer/id #uuid "b005adc1-e9e1-5b7e-a7fd-e3dc66f89bfb" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{10} & -\\sqrt{10} \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94046/selection/508c17476d336cb29bbb2b1566ed3da27c9656980a5f03a96f251a953b43a160" :answer/id #uuid "702ec27f-7777-501a-a7ac-cb418ba3c7f4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94046/selection/a3ad5b300f1839a754713fb1a736b5ab131cd367eb3b79c846970e5da67181e4" :answer/id #uuid "9a517b17-db78-573b-992d-a2242277186c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94046/selection/f4a8d545351e882b10391097afc1721a8d3c069f133f5d160893cf5707fed6f8" :answer/id #uuid "8efb7e6b-d12f-5bf3-b25f-bf0037c8c2d8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94046/selection/67dee57d669a89809f5239993863d6822e918d62678a097922fbcc75157f4059" :answer/id #uuid "2071fbdb-145c-5503-be60-44db3dc81e10" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94046/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "6f1dec07-a867-5e40-83c2-97698c3935d6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-94046/selection/f4a8d545351e882b10391097afc1721a8d3c069f133f5d160893cf5707fed6f8" :answer-field/choices ["q-94046/selection/27e238a304698a046c7e795fdbd029dfb56d969c17068e8ee401237d089bee46" "q-94046/selection/508c17476d336cb29bbb2b1566ed3da27c9656980a5f03a96f251a953b43a160" "q-94046/selection/a3ad5b300f1839a754713fb1a736b5ab131cd367eb3b79c846970e5da67181e4" "q-94046/selection/f4a8d545351e882b10391097afc1721a8d3c069f133f5d160893cf5707fed6f8" "q-94046/selection/67dee57d669a89809f5239993863d6822e918d62678a097922fbcc75157f4059"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-94046" :question/id #uuid "46cbbf2d-cd23-5aab-9cf1-375dd44b0200" :question/math-academy-id "q-94046" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe matrix $A$ above has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=10$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3.$ Find a diagonal matrix $D$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nNote that $A$ is symmetric. Therefore, it is orthogonally diagonalizable. Let's now go through the process.\n\n**Step 1.**  Notice that we already have the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=10\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-3$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=10,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-10I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 6\\end{aligned}]-10[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-10I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{13},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-3)I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 6\\end{aligned}]+3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-3)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{13},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-94046/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4754d4e6-b2d0-5efb-ac92-c004c70004df"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-94046"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-94053/selection/8d55d7ed45f25e55ec1af689915c3d10ba0fac0a7e2ccbe0ada433834ffc8370" :answer/id #uuid "64163ae4-9aa8-5e3e-a671-ca4791a0a755" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94053/selection/a8d9375bb7ccc0b2d6dda6299c46c006fdf6784388ca7deed43a10f1b129701f" :answer/id #uuid "be09f5c7-ba3a-5d07-baf7-748c9825e24e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94053/selection/df99f8ab3d5c28caf6b423bbc1e4dc8a2a223c905c871c8a23a8b292aa346dc7" :answer/id #uuid "bb44e8b5-37ab-5621-a59a-118ca14dfe23" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94053/selection/28498bec10482d72ff733c977abf3bd4285435a38e81d3cb4668cab5ca94b940" :answer/id #uuid "0a7ed7ae-7d73-5592-ade5-3c5343129de3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94053/selection/d44557bcddd8afbf73ad717c152e464f5a23ef2916ef666e9e7249de5369865d" :answer/id #uuid "613248f3-be53-5ec7-8a5b-c89309c35cae" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94053/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "5b2d42ab-ddf4-5646-9315-1e025f870cdb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-94053/selection/d44557bcddd8afbf73ad717c152e464f5a23ef2916ef666e9e7249de5369865d" :answer-field/choices ["q-94053/selection/8d55d7ed45f25e55ec1af689915c3d10ba0fac0a7e2ccbe0ada433834ffc8370" "q-94053/selection/a8d9375bb7ccc0b2d6dda6299c46c006fdf6784388ca7deed43a10f1b129701f" "q-94053/selection/df99f8ab3d5c28caf6b423bbc1e4dc8a2a223c905c871c8a23a8b292aa346dc7" "q-94053/selection/28498bec10482d72ff733c977abf3bd4285435a38e81d3cb4668cab5ca94b940" "q-94053/selection/d44557bcddd8afbf73ad717c152e464f5a23ef2916ef666e9e7249de5369865d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-94053" :question/id #uuid "b62528e2-c270-555f-b769-1e884cf1d4e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-94053" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe matrix $A$ above has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1.$ Find a diagonal matrix $D$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nNote that $A$ is symmetric. Therefore, it is orthogonally diagonalizable. Let's now go through the process.\n\n**Step 1.**  Notice that we already have the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=1\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-1$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-1I & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]-1[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-1I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-1)I & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]+1[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-1)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-94053/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4754d4e6-b2d0-5efb-ac92-c004c70004df"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-94053"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186172542 :question/answer-fields 17592186171217] {:db/id "q-94056/selection/ma-v1/6d4bac0d6af79733723a0baa38d2e7c2886a8c1ac7890fbfb6dcad29244e37f6/6eb2806d429c519de73f79024f44939ad7d355d5457c3bac1c37c60444d71383" :answer/id #uuid "391cce1c-b4d5-5af6-b4fe-7c0f8b9be058" :answer/type :an